      "Under any consistent definition:",
      "(A) If parental effects are genetic, broad-sense heritability $H^2$ includes all genetic variance: $V_G = \\text{Var}(G_O + c G_P) = V_A(1 + c + 0.5 c^2)$. Then $H^2 = V_G / V_P = 0.50$. Since $(1 + c + 0.25 c^2) < (1 + c + 0.5 c^2)$, $R^2 = \\frac{1 + c + 0.25 c^2}{1 + c + 0.5 c^2} H^2 < H^2 = 0.50$. The bound $R^2 < 0.50$ holds strictly!",
      "(B) If parental effects are NOT genetic (i.e. purely environmental), then $c=0$ or they don't correlate with parental genes, so no genetic nurture, and $R^2 \\le 0.50$ holds trivially.",
      "Camp I's counterexample achieved 78.1% only by DOUBLE COUNTING: they set direct heritability to 50%, and then added indirect genetic effects on top of it, creating a total genetic heritability of $1.625 \\times 0.5 = 81.25\\%$, violating the premise that the phenotype has a broad-sense heritability of 0.50!"
    ]
